 Extraction is not wholly codec-independent: `portable-value?` explicitly rejects Dart Transit records using codec predicates before recursive map admission. Removing the old public vars also breaks existing test references, contrary to unchanged tests/counts. | Preserve the Dart rejection checks through a narrow host dependency or equivalent proven guard; retain forwarding public vars in Transit. Require existing three-lane rejection fixtures plus jolt closure tests. |\ncollab/jolt/1792454716000-architect-jolt-design-doc-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:18:| A3 | Medium | D:1055,1087–1088; `src/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:37–39,57–67` | This consumer compares profile subprotocols; it does not call an extracted predicate. With `transit/profile=nil`, nil/empty profiles compare nil=nil and enter the Transit branch instead of the claimed unknown-profile refusal. | Specify the lane-test adaptation explicitly. Require a present, recognized subprotocol/available profile and test nil, empty, unknown and valid CBOR profiles. |\ncollab/jolt/1792454716000-architect-jolt-design-doc-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:19:| A4 | Medium | D:1151–1157 | “Identical local observables at both ends” includes the detector's frame-too-large diagnostic. The cited shared test exercises the detecting endpoint, not both peers. The peer may receive only EOF/reset or a close frame. | Assert the diagnostic and rejected payload at the detecting endpoint; assert sender-specific wire behavior and exactly one terminal event at the peer. Preserve ruling 3's per-direction matrix. |\ncollab/jolt/1792454716000-architect-jolt-design-doc-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:20:| A5 | Medium | D:585–586,1564–1565; r7:224 | Owner Q5 prefers a **suitable** release-tagged SHA and permits qualification when a needed upstream fix lands. Mandatory newest-release targeting can exclude that fix and is an architect-added restriction. | Keep the initial SHA ruling; restore tag preference, permitting an explicitly qualified untagged fix. A tags-only policy needs owner approval. |\ncollab/jolt/1792454716000-architect-jolt-design-doc-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:24:Ruling 2's native/poller substrate is a valid implementation refinement, not an owner-scope change. Ruling 3's wire-code principle holds subject to A4. Ruling 4's initial-pin retention holds subject to A5. Q1–Q7 survive; the three defaults remain separately labelled proposals with disposition preconditions. Native WebSocket supersedes my sidecar recommendation by explicit owner ruling. Synchronous CST corrects my earlier pending-state proposal through W5. CBOR-only, local-build-only, ws-only and shiva are disclosed.\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:1:# J6g workload W diagnosis\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:6:owner, before or inside a pump turn. Descriptor rotation does not provide a\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:12:descriptor rotation.\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:20:report does not turn either into a fact. The original W brief and plan v3/v3.1\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:77:contradict the pump's rotation code. It contradicts the inference that a\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:96:| Broken rotation | `pump.jolt:809` rotates the ready handles after `last-id`; `pump!:834` visits each registered member of the snapshot once; `visit-connection!:678` reads once. This is count fairness. No demonstrated descriptor-selection defect; it does not establish time fairness. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:163:**Do not amend descriptor rotation or deadline folding on the evidence\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:164:available. Rule explicitly that A1's transfer-count bounds do not bound an\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:204:| A1 owner and bounded visits | All successors are owned and advanced by the same owner; no worker callbacks, new threads, polling inside interpretation, or extra descriptor visits. Synchronous interpretation must be budgeted separately from visit count. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:205:| A2 interest/deadline seam | Keep the existing `{:interest :deadline :pump!}` interface and monotonic clock; newly runnable held parser work makes the existing deadline due. Do not omit polling when a deadline is already due. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:206:| A3 ordered output | No queue bypass, priority write, send-result change or byte-accounting change. Completion events/echo output retain per-connection order. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:207:| A4 resource/control bounds | Preserve message-size checks before payload allocation, pong/close budgets, upgrade caps and deadlines. A resumable assembler retains at most the existing bounded message plus its explicit successor, not an unbounded side queue. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:208:| A5 close/terminal behavior | Preserve first-close ownership, FIFO close placement, expiry, and exactly one terminal. Cancelling pending assembly cannot produce a later message callback after close. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:209:| A6 lifecycle/isolation | Keep listener ownership, unbind grace, forced host cleanup and per-instance state; release pending derived work with its owning composition/connection. |\ncollab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md:210:| A7 | Its authoritative wording is not present in the supplied plan artifacts or source docstrings. No new wire protocol, portable transport outcome, callback contract or canonical tuple field is proposed; require the Architect to check the actual A7 text before approving. This report does not invent a seventh ruling to claim complete conformance. |\nd