{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,130p' docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md && rg -n 'defn|published|snapshot|pending|manifest|coverage' src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc | tail -65 && sed -n '1150,1210p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc && sed -n '1640,1695p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,130p' docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md && rg -n 'defn|published|snapshot|pending|manifest|coverage' src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc | tail -65 && sed -n '1150,1210p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc && sed -n '1640,1695p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"# yin.repl: automatic code indexing\n\nStatus: design seam; the owner has ruled automatic indexing on evaluation.\nThe `q` surface is user-require by owner ruling (2026-09-27).\n\n## Composition\n\n`yin.repl/make-session` creates `program-out` and keeps its writer as\n`:row-stream` and the evaluator's independent reader as `:row-observer`\n(`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:523-554`). The expander appends one expanded\n`[root rows]` packet there for each forwarded program\n(`yin.vm.macro.md`, section 2.4); `run-evaluator-stage` reads it at\n`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:767-779`. Attach a second, independently\nadvanced observer to this same medium when building the session. After\n`run-expander-stage` forwards a packet, advance the index observer for\nthat packet as part of the evaluation round. Do not put indexing in a\nloader, runner, frontend, or the link pair. The link pair serves content\nfor `require`; it is not the evaluated-program stream.\n\nThe observer consumes packets whether the VM later returns, parks, or\nraises. A failed expansion forwards no packet, so it contributes no\nexpanded code. Give the index reader the same gap and reset discipline as\nthe evaluator reader: a lost packet must be reported, not called indexed.\nReset and VM selection rebuild the session and its observer together.\n\n## Indexed facts\n\nThe packet's root is the program's content address; each canonical flat\nrow `[id tag & slots]` has its own content-addressed node id\n(`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, sections 4 and 6.1). Maintain the `$ast`\nrow relation and its derived occurrence relation from these rows. This\nis the direct input used by `dao.space.query/q` in the free-names tests\n(`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1121-1161`). Do not relabel these\nvariable-arity rows as five-slot datoms.\n\nFor the `dao.space.index` transactor's covered indexes, project the same\nexpanded tree to local `[e a v t m]` AST facts as specified in\n`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, section 6.5. Its `e` is a transactor-local\nentity id, with `:yin/address` pointing back to the node address; it is\nnot the node identity. Commit a program's facts as one atomic transaction\nrecord, `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`. The transactor\nallocates `t`; the shell never supplies a clock. Attribute queries and\n`pull` use this projection; positional structural queries use `$ast`.\n\nThe session that minted the evaluation is provenance. Record its stable\nshell token as a fact on a metadata entity and refer to that entity in\nthe datoms' `m` slot, following the metadata-entity convention; `m` is a\nlocal integer, not a session string. Preserve the root/round association\noutside the content-hashed rows. Index code and its claims only: printed\noutput, last-value history, and ephemeral evaluation results do not enter\nthe code index by default.\n\n**Rows every round** (owner decision of 2026-10-01,\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1). After a program's transaction commits, the\nindexer materializes every row of its expanded tree into the index store\n(`yin.vm/materialize-tree!`), before the round publishes, so a\n`:yin/address` fact names content the store holds and a peer can load a\nprogram's tree by its `:yin.repl/root` address. A row put that fails is\nthe round's failure at stage `:materialize`, and the round does not\npublish. The committed program's row set stays unwritten: every later\nround that commits (and every name transaction) writes the unwritten rows\nfirst, oldest first, and publishes only when none remain, so HEAD never\nmoves over a missing row. When they are all written the failure is\ncleared, the publication covers every committed transaction, and the\nround says the index caught up. Restored history is not re-materialized.\n\n**Names are claims in the same index** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.1).\n`(yin.link/publish 'm '[exports])` commits the signed name envelopes as\none transaction — one entity per envelope carrying\n`:yin.module/envelope` and `:yin.module/proof`, the session's metadata\nentity (no program root) in `m` — and publishes at once, so the round's\nHEAD write announces the module's blobs with the index naming them.\n\n## Query surface: owner ruling (2026-09-27)\n\n**User require.** `dao.space.query/q` is not bound in the session; the\nuser's own `(require 'dao.space.query)` binds it, running over the\nindex this observer maintains. Ruled for `yin.repl`'s explicit, minimal\nshell contract. Binding `q` at the first prompt (the IDE posture) was\nconsidered and is a composition a host can still make; the shell does\nnot. Automatic indexing is a session composition\npolicy; exposing an extra language binding is a separate policy. The\nowner can choose the first-prompt posture without changing the medium,\ntransaction format, or index observer.\n\n## Cost and boundary\n\nStartup adds one observer attachment and a local index/transactor value;\nthere is no eager scan of program history or remote fetch. An idle\nsession performs no indexing work. Each forwarded evaluation pays a\nlinear pass over its expanded rows, one atomic local transaction for the\ndatom projection, and the index update; `q` pays its own query cost when\ncalled. This work is bounded by that program's size and is acceptable as\nthe cost of making every evaluated program queryable. Publication of\ncovered indexes can follow the index's normal cadence, outside the\nevaluation critical path.\n\nThis is neither ShiBi authorization nor the `dao.jing.content` service\nin `dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1. It adds no\nwire shape. It composes beside that epic's middleware, remote channel,\ncontent service, REPL service, UDP, pair, and UCF slices; their transport\nand content-service file sets need no change for this local observer.\n\n## Durable store: startup, HEAD, and the directory lock\n\nThe store is picked once, at startup: `--index-store mem | file:<dir>`\nthrough the one shared argument parser every host's `-main` uses, or\n`:index-store-spec` (`:mem`, `{:type :file :dir dir}`) to\n`yin.repl/create-state`. Omission means `mem`, today's behaviour. A\nmissing value, an unknown scheme, an empty directory, a directory that\ncannot be opened, a host build without file support, a handle and a spec\ntogether — each refuses before any shell or server composes, with its\nreason; nothing falls back to memory silently. There is no runtime\nswitching.\n\nThe durable directory store is `dao.space.store` (its host file\noperations, the lock among them, are `dao.space.store.fs`): the shell is\none consumer through `yin.repl.store`, and `dao.space.dht/join {:dir …}`\nis another, so the lock below holds between REPLs and plain Clojure\nnodes alike.\n\nA durable directory holds three things: `content.jing` — the\n`dao.jing.file` content log the indexer's publications materialize into,\nexactly as the memory store receives them; `HEAD` — a versioned record\n(`{:version 1 :manifest <address>}`, EDN) naming the latest published\nmanifest; and `lock` — the exclusive lock. Opening is exclusive and\nvalidating: the lock is acquired before anything else in the directory\nis touched and held until the store closes; a second owner is refused\nnaming the directory, and the first is unaffected. The JVM and Dart take\nan operating-system lock on `lock` (released when the holding process\n363:(defn make-pair\n382:(defn- refusal\n387:(defn- refused?\n392:(defn- unavailable\n400:(defn- invalid\n405:(defn- snapshot\n406:  \"The database a call reads, or a refusal: the latest manifest the\n407:   indexer published, read back from its store.  Committed code must all\n408:   be published, so no older manifest stands in for the current one.\"\n410:  (let [{:keys [lost? failure published? transactions]}\n423:      (not published?)\n424:      (unavailable \"committed code is not published\")\n429:                                           (:manifest-address indexer)))\n431:          (unavailable (str \"the published index could not be read: \"\n435:(defn- ast-relations\n457:(defn- byte-length\n462:(defn- encoded-size\n470:(defn- in-patterns\n485:(defn- caller-patterns\n492:(defn- ast-patterns\n498:(defn- split-args\n515:(defn- with-index\n533:(defn- view-of\n549:(defn- row-count\n558:(defn- bounded\n596:(defn- evaluate\n601:(defn answer\n631:                             (snapshot indexer))\n648:(defn- host-status\n658:(defn- refusable-call\n659:  \"`[node' answer]` of the plain function `f` on `node` and `manifest`:\n662:  [node manifest f ok]\n663:  (try (let [node (f node manifest)] [node {:ok (ok node)}])\n670:(defn- publication-call\n671:  \"`[node' answer]` of `retry!` or `cancel!` on `manifest`: `ok` answers\n673:  [node manifest f ok]\n674:  (refusable-call node manifest f (constantly ok)))\n677:(defn- dht-answer\n691:        [manifest & more] (apply2/request-args request)\n701:          (not (jing/segment-address? manifest))\n702:          [node (invalid (str \"dao.space.dht expects a manifest address, got \"\n703:                              (pr-str manifest)))]\n708:            (refusable-call node manifest dht/load-index\n709:                            #(:status (dht/load-status % manifest)))\n712:            [node {:ok (host-status (dht/load-status node manifest))}]\n715:            (refusable-call node manifest linker.dht/load-module\n716:                            #(:status (linker.dht/module-status % manifest)))\n719:            [node {:ok (host-status (linker.dht/module-status node manifest))}]\n722:            (publication-call node manifest dht/retry! :retrying)\n725:            (publication-call node manifest dht/cancel! :cancelled)\n728:            (let [status (dht/load-status node manifest)\n731:                      (bounded #(apply dht/q node manifest query inputs) limits)\n733:                               (str \"the index \" manifest \" is not loaded (\"\n735:                                    \"); (dao.space.dht/load-index \" manifest\n743:(defn- dht-op?\n748:(defn- head-op?\n753:(defn- link-op?\n758:(defn- session-modules\n760:   it linked through the linker, `{name manifest-address}` -- a linked\n761:   entry carries its manifest's address -- and the names of its host\n769:(defn- link-answer\n774:   `yin.vm.linker.dht/publish-name!` over the published index, whose\n798:                     (snapshot indexer))]\n845:(defn- head-answer\n874:(defn serve\n   replayed — by the next input.  Output the round already emitted is\n   drained here, once.\"\n  [state error]\n  (let [carried (get-in (ex-data error) [:session :observer])\n        resume (fn [observer]\n                 (if (and observer\n                          carried\n                          (= (:stream carried) (:stream observer)))\n                   carried\n                   observer))\n        drained (assoc state\n                       :observer (drain-observer (resume (:observer state)))\n                       :row-observer (drain-observer\n                                       (resume (:row-observer state)))\n                       :indexer (index/skip (:indexer state))\n                       :ast-indexer (ast-index/skip (:ast-indexer state)))\n        [state' output-text]\n        (drain-output\n          (cond-> drained\n            (> (ingress-gaps drained) (ingress-gaps state))\n            (assoc :ingress-loss? true)))]\n    [state' (str output-text (format-error error))]))\n\n\n(defn- run-index-stage\n  \"Drive the code indexer's observer over `program-out`: it commits each\n   forwarded packet as one transaction and publishes the covered indexes.\n   It runs before the evaluator, so a program is indexed whether its VM\n   returns, parks, or raises; the two observers meet only at the medium.\"\n  [state]\n  (update state :indexer index/step (:round state)))\n\n\n(defn- run-ast-index-stage\n  \"Drive the AST indexer's observer over `program-out`: it adds each\n   forwarded packet's rows and occurrences to the session's `$ast` and\n   `$occ` relations.  It runs before the evaluator for the same reason the\n   code indexer does, and neither indexer reads the other.\"\n  [state]\n  (update state :ast-indexer ast-index/step))\n\n\n(defn- with-index-notice\n  \"Append what the round's index steps lost, if anything, to its result\n   text, one line per notice in the order given; the evaluation's own\n   answer is unchanged.\"\n  [[state text] notices]\n  (if-let [notice (some->> (remove nil? notices) seq (str/join \"\\n\"))]\n    [state (if (str/blank? text) notice (str text \"\\n\" notice))]\n    [state text]))\n\n\n(defn- run-expander-stage\n  \"Drive the expander over `program-in` and drain its summary.  It expands\n   each observed batch and appends the tree packet to `program-out`; a\n   failed expansion forwards nothing, yet its cursor advances (yin.vm.macro.md\n   decision 12).  Returns the state with the expander's progress and the\n   drained `{:errors :forwarded}` summary.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [{:keys [observer consumer]}\n        (macro/step {:observer (:observer state), :consumer (:expander state)})\n   would report a result built on programs the VM never saw.  The loss is\n   reported and the shell refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`.\n\n   A completed program's lexical environment does not outlive it: the VM's\n   environment is restored to the one the round began with, as `vm/eval`\n   does.  A round that throws is consumed by `consume-failed-round`.\"\n  [state program]\n  (if (:ingress-loss? state)\n    [state (str \"Error: \" ingress-loss-text)]\n    (let [state' (update (inject-last-value state) :round inc)\n          batch (encoder/program-batch program)\n          append (stream/append! (:program-stream state') batch)]\n      (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome append))\n        [state (str \"Error: program batch not ingested: \"\n                    (name (:dao.stream/outcome append)))]\n        (let [expanded (try\n                         (run-expander-stage state')\n                         (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                                   :clj Exception\n                                   :cljs js/Error) error\n                           {:failed (consume-failed-round state error)}))]\n          (if-let [failed (:failed expanded)]\n            failed\n            (let [[expanded {:keys [errors forwarded]}] expanded\n                  indexed (cond-> expanded\n                            (pos? forwarded) (-> run-index-stage\n                                                 run-ast-index-stage))]\n              (with-index-notice\n                (cond\n                  (> (ingress-gaps indexed) (ingress-gaps state'))\n                  [(assoc indexed :ingress-loss? true)\n                   (str \"Error: \" ingress-loss-text)]\n\n                  (pos? forwarded) (run-evaluation state indexed)\n\n                  :else [(assoc indexed :vm (:vm state))\n                         (format-expansion-errors errors)])\n                (when (pos? forwarded)\n                  [(index/notice (:indexer expanded) (:indexer indexed))\n                   (ast-index/notice (:ast-indexer indexed))])))))))))\n\n\n(defn- installed-text\n  \"The languages and ids `catalog` holds, for a refused selection to name:\n   a name that is neither is a typo, not a missing frontend.\"\n  [catalog]\n  (let [entries (:yang.frontend/entries catalog)\n        listed (fn [xs] (str/join \" \" (map pr-str (sort (distinct xs)))))]\n    (str \"installed: \"\n         (listed (map (comp :yang.frontend/language :manifest) (vals entries)))\n         \" (ids \" (listed (map first (keys entries))) \")\")))\n\n\n(defn- select-frontend\n  \"Select the frontend `lang` names in `catalog`: a qualified id selects\n   by id (ambiguous when several revisions are installed), `[id revision]`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2000,2075p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md && sed -n '1,85p' src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/crypto.jolt && rg --files | rg '(BRIEF-j6g|websocket.*v3|ws.*v3|plan-v3|j6-plan)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2000,2075p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md && sed -n '1,85p' src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/crypto.jolt && rg --files | rg '(BRIEF-j6g|websocket.*v3|ws.*v3|plan-v3|j6-plan)'\"","aggregated_output":"```\n\nRestore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\nEvery kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n\nNo completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n\nA fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n\n\n### 7.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\n\nAn exclusive running, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only. A completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write. Completed outcomes never acquire publication or execution authority from their history.\n\n1. **Preemption under live tenure.** `run-cycle` uses `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under its existing gate and halted guards. Holder `:fuel` is a positive integer or nil, checked by `check-common!`. Nil retains unrestricted behavior. After replay, emit and live read, active control leaves the holder running with `:yin.k/at-boundary true` in its status detail. The composition explicitly calls `hand-off`; no automatic export at the bound is implied.\n\n2. **Tenure dies after a completed slice.** Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\n   Each stage checks tenure against its input, executes with the existing external-seam checks, associates its returned machine and metadata, and passes that successor to the next stage. Its outcome is `{:status :ok :state completed-state}` or `{:status :failed :state latest-completed-state :failure failure}`. The stage-local catch closes over its input. The failure consumer calls `end-run` on that carried state; `:yin.k/retained-boundary` records its PC, segment, checkpoint and completed-slice count (`:yin.k/steps-since-checkpoint`, holder program steps, not a guest-instruction count). A failure before an operation returns retains the previous completed stage, never an invented partial result. External effects are not rolled back. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their propagation rules.\n\n3. **Expiry and recovery.** Progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it. Recovery lowers the checkpoint this holder received, or a durably authorized successor. Retained local progress receives no adoption, execution or publication authority. The source remains ended; `exit-mint` and abort cannot revive its tenure.\n\n4. **Reopen and fencing.** Direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` refuse `:exporting` and `:ended` before guest, scheduler or bridge work. The guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop. The refusal is an ex-info whose data is `{:rule :gate-closed :yin.k/gate mode}`. A fenced running restore preserves control, window, environment, K and ordered completed outcomes for byte-identical relift; nothing runs during lower. `rehydrate-fenced` reproduces the published pack byte for byte, including cursor updates.\n\n\n### 7.4 Fuel\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n\nOne invocation performs:\n\n1. Closed-gate check.\n2. Exactly one raw budgeted action:\n   - If control is present, `vm-hot` with fuel `n`.\n   - Otherwise, one budgeted scheduler round.\n3. If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one `ffi/bridge-step`.\n4. Return that resulting machine immediately.\n\nThe implementation does **not** wrap the raw action in unchanged `ffi/maybe-run`. A handled response or malformed-request diagnostic does not restart the runner or bridge inside the same invocation. The next caller invocation resumes progress.\n\nThe bounded bridge helper may be private to the semantic runner or a bounded variant beside `ffi/maybe-run`; its contract is the four steps above. Existing unrestricted `vm/run` bridge behavior remains unchanged for live machines.\n\n**Budgeted scheduler round.**\n\nPropagate the budget through `check-wait-set`, `poll-links`, `advance-installs` and `advance-install`. Each visited child is invoked once through `run-fuel child n`; nested children use the same rule. Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates.\n\nAfter the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation. A running child retains phase `:running`; blocked children retain `:parked`; halted children complete installation as today.\n\nThe dispatch tests **control presence**, not merely `engine/active-continuation?`: the latter only tests blocked/halted flags and can classify a control-less ingress-ready machine as active. Therefore no `run-fuel` path falls through `vm-hot` into its unbudgeted scheduler fallback. A quiescent control-less machine takes the budgeted no-work round and returns.\n\n**Per-tree bound.**\n\nEach task visited during the invocation receives at most one budgeted action and at most one bridge step. Each task executes at most `n` guest instructions. For `t` distinct tasks visited during the invocation, including children created during the round, the instruction bound is `n × t`. No task is revisited with a fresh budget inside that invocation.\n\n**The honest guarantee**, copied verbatim into the documents:\n\n> Fuel counts completed guest instructions. One `run-fuel` invocation executes at most `n` instructions per visited task, at most one scheduler round per visited inactive task, and at most one bridge step per visited task. A bridge result never restarts execution inside that invocation. No wall-clock bound is claimed for an instruction, host function, stream constructor, variable resolution, bridge step or scheduler sweep. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness and the composition chooses `n`.\n\n`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n\nRunning install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n\n\n## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n\nVerification follows `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: G0 per iteration on\nthe JVM, G1 `bb test:sub yin.vm` on three hosts, G2 `bb test:changed`,\nG3 on master.\n\n### 8.1 Slice 6a: the frame validator and the row codec (independent)\n\n**Files to create.**\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc` (§3): `defect`, public.\n(ns dao.stream.ws.crypto\n  \"The jolt-only digest and entropy the RFC 6455 core needs, injected into\n   it by the jolt adapter (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §9;\n   J6 plan v2 A5, A7). The portable core takes both as arguments and never\n   reaches a host; this namespace is where jolt's come from.\n\n   `sha1` is the Sec-WebSocket-Accept digest of the opening handshake\n   (RFC 6455 §4.2.2) and nothing else: SHA-1 is not a content, integrity or\n   identity hash anywhere in the system (those are BLAKE3, `dao.host.hash`).\n   `mask-key` draws the four masking-key bytes of a client frame (§5.3).\n\n   Both call the native/jolt-crypto wrapper (layout 4: `jolt_crypto_sha1`,\n   EVP_sha1 from the system libcrypto, and `jolt_crypto_random`, its\n   RAND_bytes), the same wrapper and the same qualified library every\n   `dao.host.hash` and `dao.host.sign` call resolved to. This namespace\n   requires `dao.host.hash` first, so its load-time gate (layout, then\n   provenance over all twenty libcrypto entries) has passed before any\n   symbol here is bound or any byte digested or drawn; a stale, missing or\n   unqualified wrapper, or one lacking either entry, fails the load. A failed digest or draw throws\n   (ex-info), never a silent substitute.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    ;; loaded for its gate, which must pass before anything below binds\n    [dao.host.hash]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n\n\n(def ^:private library\n  ;; the wrapper dao.host.hash loaded and qualified (jolt's os.name and\n  ;; os.arch shim can answer nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64)\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")\n        darwin? (str/includes? os \"mac\")]\n    (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\"\n         (if darwin? \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\")\n         \"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\" (if darwin? \".dylib\" \".so\"))))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n\n\n;; defcfn binds lazily (a missing entry surfaces only at the first call), so\n;; the load refuses here, before guest work, when an entry is absent\n(doseq [entry [\"jolt_crypto_sha1\" \"jolt_crypto_random\"]]\n  (when-not (ffi/find-symbol entry)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"jolt-crypto refused: the library lacks \" entry)\n                    {:library library, :entry entry}))))\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sha1 \"jolt_crypto_sha1\"\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-random \"jolt_crypto_random\"\n            [:pointer :size_t] :int :blocking)\n\n\n(defn- with-native\n  \"(f p) over caller-owned native memory `p` of `n` bytes (one for an empty\n   input), freed however `f` exits.\"\n  [n f]\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\n    (try\n      (when (ffi/null? p)\n        (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\" {:bytes n})))\n      (f p)\n      (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n\n\n(defn sha1\n  \"The 20-byte SHA-1 digest of host bytes `bs`, as fresh host bytes; `bs`\n   is left unmodified. For Sec-WebSocket-Accept only.\"\n  [bs]\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\n    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.ws.crypto/sha1 expects host bytes\" {:value bs})))\n  (let [n (alength bs)]\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [in]\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array in bs))\n        (with-native 20\n          (fn [out]\n            (let [status (c-sha1 in n out)]\n              (when-not (zero? status)\nBRIEF-j6g-fix.md\ntest/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
